<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jige</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as A-frame</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A jige is a traditional Korean pack frame for carrying heavy loads on the back. Used for centuries in farming communities, it carried compost, grain, wood and grass, and its easy making and the mountainous Korean terrain made it widespread; designs varied by region. The name jige is first known from a book of 1690 and was established by the 18th century. Its frame of pine wood stands on two legs with upward-angled branches to hold the load, straw shoulder straps and a loose stick for propping it up and walking. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> of 1950–1953 it still carried supplies and ammunition in the mountains, and American soldiers called it the A-frame.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Each part of a jige has its own name, and the names differ between the dialects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. The narrow top is the saegojari. Four or five crosspieces, the saejang, join the two sides, held together by a rope, the tanggae-jul, tightened with a wooden peg, the tanggae-mok; a woodcutter could hang his sickle on the rope. The gaji are the wooden branches that support the load, the mokbal are the feet, and the milppi are the straw straps. The jige-jakdaegi is the loose stick used to stand the frame up and as a walking stick on slopes.</p><p>The triangle formed by the legs and the stick makes the loaded frame stable, and the shoulders and the back of the frame act as a fulcrum, so that the load is lifted with less effort. The legs are loosely fixed so that the frame can be fitted to the carrier's height. A heavy load had to be well balanced and carried at a steady pace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The common je-gaji jige needs two branches of similar angle and thickness as its gaji; its body is of pine and its crosspieces of hard woods such as birch or chestnut. Frames used on the plains were longer than those of the mountains, where narrow paths made falls more likely. The short geo jige was carried on the back of a cow. The ba jige, without gaji, was used by carriers who crossed the mountains inland from the coast of Gyeongsang Province. The mul jige and georeum jige carried buckets of water or manure on a pole across the shoulders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Similar frames are found elsewhere. The Chinese bei-jia, used in the northern mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, differs in its gaji. Frames hung from the forehead by a rope are used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the Pima and Papago peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> used carrying frames called kihos. According to the Encyclopedia of Korean Culture, the jige was used on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsushima-island">Tsushima Island</a> under a form of its Korean name, though some Japanese scholars hold that it developed there on its own.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jige” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
